From 0d10252520278b52e0e6964b911cc7257a5c63c5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:38:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Desktop read sharing: key on the native grant;
 WS-RPC session writes start a new generation

Follow-up to #2498 (shared in-flight backend reads, 9deeb8c84e), from review t_01a1d61a3a3363f1's
non-blocking behaviour notes (card t_01a1d621b1af9854).

1. The read key named only whether a descriptor had a native grant (Boolean(gatewayEndpoint)). A
   multiplexer serves several profiles behind one api_origin, each with its own grant, and an
   unscoped read answers for the grant's profile: two such descriptors would share one wire request
   and both get the first profile's answer. The key now carries the grant's identity
   (profile_id, instance_id, authority_epoch).
2. Only REST writes through fetchJsonForBackend started a new generation. Session mutations the
   renderer sends over the gateway WebSocket (session.title / archive / compress / rewind / model /
   branch, policy edits, session.create) never pass that sink, so a list refetch right after a
   rename could join a list GET sent before it and show the old title. HermesGateway.request now
   announces each such write to main (preload noteBackendWrite -> ipc hermes:backend:note-write ->
   ReadCoalescer.noteWriteEverywhere) as it leaves and again once it settles (refusals included).
   The renderer does not know which backend origin main resolves for a WS connection, so the notice
   starts a new generation on every backend; writes are rare user actions, so the cost is a few
   unshared reads right after one.
3. A REST write now also starts a new generation when it settles, not only when it is sent: a read
   sent while the write was in flight may hold the old state, and a read issued after the write's
   answer must not join it (same read-after-write gap as 2).

Tests
- electron/read-coalescing.test.ts: multiplexed grants differing in profile_id / instance_id /
  authority_epoch (and grant vs none) never share, an equal grant still does; a read after a write
  settles does not join one sent during it; noteWriteEverywhere separates reads on every backend
  while reads after it still share; main/preload wiring of the notice.
- src/api/backend-write-notice.test.ts: a real ws fixture: session.title / session.archive notify
  before the session.mutate frame and after its answer, session.resume does not; a refused
  mutation still notifies after it settles; the write-method set.
- RED on origin/main: 4 of the new electron tests and both ws-fixture tests fail; GREEN with the fix.
  Hand mutants (14: each grant field, the settle bump, the everywhere generation, each notice, the
  method set, the ipc and preload lines) are all killed.
- main.ts stays under its FILE_LINES cap: the profile-name extraction of the native all-profiles
  path moved to profile-session-routing.ts as profileNamesOf (same filter; unit-tested).
- src/api (42 files, 265 tests) and electron (413 files) pass; the one electron failure under the
  scratch config (updater/checkout-source) needs the project's Python globalSetup and passes under
  --project electron. tsc (tsconfig.json, tsconfig.electron.json) and eslint clean.
